<section class="pipeline-section" id="alerts"><div class="shell"><div class="section-title"><p class="overline">Alert model</p><h2>Condition, threshold,<br>signal, notification.</h2><p>A monitoring target produces measurements on a schedule. A condition compares a measurement to a threshold. When it is crossed, a signal event is recorded, an incident is opened, and a notification is sent. Every step is a row in the database, so the history is real and re-readable.</p></div>
<ol class="engine-path" aria-label="Alert flow">{% for step in ["Condition","Threshold","Signal","Notification"] %}<li><span class="project-type">{{ loop.index0 | pad }}</span><strong>{{ step }}</strong></li>{% endfor %}</ol>
<pre class="vector-console" aria-label="Example rule">IF uptime &lt; 99% over 24h
THEN open incident, notify, record event</pre>
<p class="fine-print">Build order: website uptime and response time first, then SSL expiry, then historical data, then alerts. Python scheduled workers run the checks; PostgreSQL stores targets, signals, and events. This dimension goes live when the first real check has run, not before.</p></div></section>
